[verify 5] innerHTML user-input write check (hard rule)
$ git grep -nE "\.innerHTML\s*=\s*['\"\`]" index.html
(no output, exit 1 = no matches)
exit 1 (= no matches = OK)

Explanation: the previous index.html used `el.innerHTML = list.map(renderArticle).join('')`,
which built DOM via template-literal interpolation of user-derived fields (a.title, a.author,
a.url). Those fields come from the PTT scraper in api/tracker.js; the original code stripped
tags but did not escape all HTML, so any residual `<script>`, `onerror=`, or `"javascript:"`
payload from PTT content could execute in the page.

Refactor: renderList() now clears the list with while-loop removeChild() and builds every
article via document.createElement + textContent / setAttribute. No string concatenation
reaches innerHTML. Article text goes through textContent; href goes through .href (the URL
setter, not innerHTML). This is the canonical XSS-safe DOM pattern and removes the innerHTML
risk entirely.

Note on the grep regex: `\.\s*=\s*['\"\`]` matches `el.innerHTML = 'literal'` style writes.
`element.innerHTML = element` style assignments (rare) are not matched, but our codebase has
none (`git grep innerHTML index.html app.js` returns exit 1, i.e. zero occurrences anywhere).

